# The Way of the Wheel!
Tamil title: சக்கர நெறி!

In the Kalinga war, Emperor Ashoka truly achieved a magnificent victory, greater than any king had known after even the greatest battle. The sacred principle that war must cease became deeply rooted in his mind. That was his supreme victory, one no other had attained or could attain.

Any king rejoices when his army wins a fierce war. A less discerning one grows proud. A shrewd king considers how victory may arouse jealousy in other rulers, create new enemies, inspire secret conspiracy in the defeated land, or tempt his own commanders to seize the crown in pride over their prowess. A pleasure-mad king orders wine, women and song; a devout one visits temples. Ashoka did none of these. He won, then asked: How, over what, and for what was this victory?

Thoughts fought within him more fiercely than the enemy on the battlefield. At last he achieved the greater victory and discovered the new truth that war must cease and triumph in war is no triumph. His inner struggle was immense. Courage cried: “If you reject war, other kings will call you a coward and mock that you sing hymns because your shoulders have lost their strength.” Wisdom let courage speak, then soothed him: “Do not grieve, king. Other rulers may mock you, but if you remain armed for war, corpses will mock you: ‘You came to rule, yet make us die. Is this government?’ Orphans will mock you upon your decorated throne. Can you endure that?”

The country praised the conqueror of Kalinga. Victory flags touched the sky and songs celebrating him floated on the air. Everyone imagined no one could be happier than Ashoka after such a victory. The realm celebrated, but the king would not join. The people's eyes shone with valour and victory; Ashoka's held sorrow.

Ministers asked why he sat bowed in deep thought like one who had lost something when Kalinga had fallen. Another voice within answered: “Was it only Kalinga that fell?”

The city held triumphant soldiers and smiling women garlanding them, but also men without arms and legs and lovers grieving beside them. A minister described how the warrior Gunanathan, surrounded by 150 cavalrymen, drew his sword without fear, sent Kalinga heads rolling, became like many warriors at once, broke the encirclement and returned victorious. He proposed presenting him a gem-studded shield. Ashoka gave it without joy or emotion. Those around him feared that the great war had shocked his mind. They were right, but the shock came not merely from battle's severity; it came after the drums announced victory.

“Victory! Victory!” they thundered, striking the king's mind like hammers. The word seemed to laugh at him. The blinded, the armless, the legless, those crushed under horses and elephants, corpses picked by great birds, and bodies mangled beneath chariot wheels appeared before him, all crying “Victory!”

“Victory for whom?” he repeatedly asked himself. Is destruction victory? Is standing beside a mound of bodies victory? If obtaining what one sought is victory, a gardener may rejoice when a new flower blooms. But I destroyed what existed, spread the stench of corpses, and feel none of the gardener's joy. Is this victory?

Sleep would not come. The sword stirred excitement; its bloodstain made his heart tremble. Subdued Kalinga inspired pride; the piled bodies laid a great burden upon him. Royal qualities thought to be valour, honour, hunger for territory, love of domination and splendour resisted death within him. After the fierce inner war, the conqueror reached his decision: no more war.

An artist, unaware of this struggle, poured all his skill into a painting glorifying the conquest. He corrected it from every angle until sweat fell into his colours. When his work and Ashoka's inner battle were both finished, he took the scroll into the council chamber where the king had summoned ministers to announce his discovery.

The artist bowed and offered his picture of the victory at Kalinga, explaining that he had used all his skill to portray imperial greatness and the king's triumph. Ashoka repeated “My victory at Kalinga?” with irony, looked at the painting and set it aside with a faint smile. The artist, wounded by unprecedented neglect, took up his scroll like a mother whose child had been scorned and sat there unable to leave.

Ashoka returned with another picture. “Here is the true form of Kalinga: massacre, a flood of blood, heaps of bodies, vultures circling above. Those dead upon the field were killed by me. Ashoka's victory means wailing in thousands of homes. Open slaughter is called war. So many corpses for my fame, so much blood for my kingdom: a realm of the dead!

“Artist, do not make art a screen. Do not conceal massacre. Paint me if you wish, but not with a flower garland. Give me a garland of skulls, and beneath it write in red, ‘Ashoka, who slaughtered without fear.’ Let the world mock this devourer of life. There shall be no more war. A king's prowess need not be established by conquering another country. If kingship requires that, Ashoka will cease being king. If government serves destruction, Ashoka wants no more rule.”

A minister softly observed that the king's fame had spread everywhere. Ashoka answered: “Ask the mother who lost the son who fought for me. Ask the lover who learnt vultures picked at her beloved. They will ask whether their families must suffer so the emperor may gain fame and whether their lives must darken so his crown may shine.”

The minister called such speech treason. Ashoka replied: “Sending people to die in war in the country's name is treason against humanity. It is neither dharma, love nor wisdom, but animal nature, bloodlust, hunger for domination and the mind of a demon rejoicing over corpses. Enough. I no longer want fame from war. Young warriors need not be wounded to bring me glory. I will wage war against ignorance and poverty, make knowledge my weapon and love my army. I want no new kingdoms; I want new joy upon people's faces. I have sent armies in every direction; hereafter I will send teachers. I have stopped the war drum; love and virtue shall be my drum.”

Ashoka had never spoken with such feeling. The artist stared in alarm and ministers stood open-mouthed. The king held his head in silence, dismissed the assembly and went inside.

He issued new commands consistent with his discovery and the Buddhist way, though not designed to exterminate other faiths:

- No more war.
- No hunting.
- No meat for palace feasts.
- Dharma must flourish in the country.

People initially wondered why a ruler needed to command virtue, since no government openly supported vice. The explanation followed: officials would be appointed to teach dharma. These were not hereditary claimants, forest-hut pretenders or costumed impostors, but learned and disciplined people charged with spreading a good path. They did not use office to seek domination; they cleansed impurities in people's minds.

Hereditary orthodox authorities became genuinely afraid that Ashoka's plan undermined their dominance. Another order intensified their anger: there must be no killing of living beings and no sacrificial slaughter.

They lamented that rights enjoyed for generations, honour and influence had vanished. The king had forbidden sacrifice. They called him a reviler of the Vedas, a great sinner who joined a heretical faith, and complained that the people joined his course. They invoked “As the king, so the subjects,” mourning that caste custom, hereditary duty and varnasrama would lose respect. They insisted the Vedas contained the beginning and end of knowledge, were eternal, and that they themselves had been born to study them and make the world obey them. Ashoka boldly refused this “truth.” They wished to call Buddhism madness, but feared the public would laugh them to death.

At last they went to challenge him: who was he to alter an ancient system and by what authority?

Ashoka answered that he was king of everyone, not Brahmins alone. All subjects were equal before him. As a follower of the virtuous Buddha, he could not grant privilege by declaring a few superior by birth. In his dharma realm everyone was equal and worthy of his love, including animals. He had forbidden hunting for pleasure even in forests. Yet they demanded the law of the jungle in the country. Ashoka was Buddha's servant and no one else's slave. He would absolutely prevent animals being killed in the name of sacrifice; that was his law.

After speaking at length of hereditary entitlement and ancient practice, the orthodox deputation had no answer and departed bewildered, believing the realm no longer useful to them.

Ashoka next summoned a sculptor and asked him to create work through which his art would endure. The sculptor eagerly asked where a temple should be built. “Not a temple,” said the king. “God already has millions; are not people's hearts his loftiest temples?”

The king ordered stupas bearing teachings of dharma throughout the empire. Kingdoms perish, he said, but virtue must endure, and art should serve that end. Not forts and battlements, but pillars teaching dharma. He supplied the necessary resources, blessing art and virtue.

Until then governments knew only how to dispatch armies. Ashoka's sculptors became commanders of a new kind, raising pillars everywhere so other lands might benefit, not tremble. Compared with this, Kalinga was a grain-sized victory. His teachers travelled through many regions and overseas at rulers' requests, winning vast victories without war. Instead of pointing to cities and forts a king had destroyed, people proudly said, “Here is Ashoka's stupa.”

Not everyone rejoiced. Orthodox groups, unable openly to vent their anger that the old order had gone, muttered that this was no king and no palace: there were no dances, feasts or splendour, only the appearance of a cremation ground. Though the realm reached the Narmada and the treasury overflowed, an ascetic sat upon the throne. He built no temples, held no consecrations or festivals, but endlessly told people not to kill deer or peacocks, lie, eat flesh, or abandon love and virtue. Other kings enjoyed water sports with celestial beauties and royal feasts; this one lacked only the begging bowl to complete an ascetic's costume. He claimed the Kalinga war had opened his eye of wisdom. They called it hypocrisy, disguise and conspiracy, grieving over lost dominance.

Ashoka neither expelled, hated nor punished them. He concentrated upon spreading his dharma realm. Ostentation disappeared. The age in which people resented palace amusements gave way to one in which they praised the palace as a temple of love and knowledge. The orthodox still called it conspiracy.

Ashoka committed no conspiracy. But some conspiracy appears to have created conditions in which his dharma realm could not survive him. History supplies no details.

Why did the path of love established across a vast empire by a distinguished emperor wither? Why did his dharma government fail after his lifetime, though stupas stood through many regions and teachers carried his path across the seas? We can still praise the Ashokan empire, emperor and nonviolent way. He gained fame no lifetime of wars like Kalinga could have won. But why did his system of government and love not remain? Why is even the identity of his successor and family line so uncertain? What destroyed the state? Why did the Buddhist path established by a ruler of such strength, overflowing love and care not to wound others disappear after him?

Artists and poets cannot answer. History must, but history remains silent. Only the most searching rational inquiry reveals even a narrow scratch of the truth: some conspiracy occurred, and the path of love was destroyed.

Will future researchers and historians discover what that “something” was? Whom shall we ask? Today Ashoka's very Dharma Chakra is the emblem upon the national flag. Should we not at least now discover the truths surrounding that symbol?

Published in *Dravida Nadu*, 8 February 1948.

---NOTES---
The Tamil OCR source is retained verbatim. It contains broken words, misplaced page furniture, and several damaged phrases, particularly in the king's long declaration after the painter scene. The English follows the recoverable syntax and argument without silently correcting the Tamil. “சக்கர நெறி” is rendered “The Way of the Wheel,” preserving its reference to Ashoka's Dharma Chakra. Names, doctrinal terms and the source's historical claims are translated as printed; they have not been independently normalised or fact-corrected. The essay's speculation about an unnamed conspiracy is preserved as the author's unresolved question, not presented as an established historical conclusion.
